## F1 re-check (withdrawn, remediated)

structured.ts imports the one exported safeModel from providers.ts
(structured.ts:12) and applies it on every console path:

- Read: servedModel = safeModel(result.model) (structured.ts:223). The raw
  response-body string reaches no variable.
- Error path: the usage record holds model: safeModel(model)
  (structured.ts:240). The logged value is the role config model, bounded
  again.
- Final path: the usage record holds model: servedModel (structured.ts:253),
  which is the validated value or null.

The charset-invalid case gives null through the same MODEL_ID regex
(providers.ts:212, providers.ts:218-219), because the helper reuses the one
validator and adds no second implementation.

I checked the remaining console paths of the structured flow. retry.ts holds
no console call. usage-log.ts prints record fields and the
role-not-configured line only (usage-log.ts:25, usage-log.ts:30);
logRoleNotConfigured receives the role literal and the env var name
(structured.ts:140, structured.ts:146). The structured client wires no debug
logger (structured.ts:150-153 passes apiKey and baseUrl only), so no SDK
debug path exists. The two BM2 callers log nothing:
spreadsheet-feedback.ts returns a typed reason or a deterministic fallback
(spreadsheet-feedback.ts:136-148), and ai-error-summary.ts returns null on
failure (ai-error-summary.ts:126-134). The raw answer string returns to the
caller as data, and no caller logs it.

The R-B1 test is failing-first: the Red log records the usage record holding
the raw key string at 36b27a50 (red-s3-r5-36b27a50.log lines 14-16). The
Green test proves the key form records model null, stays out of every console
line, and a valid model passes through. The test passed in my run.

## requireParameters non-enumerable key

The provider preference is a hardcoded literal inside the helper
(structured.ts:166-170): Object.defineProperty({ require_parameters: true },
"requireParameters", { value: true, enumerable: false }). No untrusted input
reaches it, so no injection path exists. The key holds the constant true, so
it holds no secret. Serialization skips non-enumerable keys, so the SDK
boundary keeps the outbound shape (the boundary assertion at
structured.test.ts:103-118 still passes), and the R-A1 test drives the real
SDK with a mocked fetch and proves the wire body carries
provider.require_parameters true (structured.test.ts:236-296). Both tests
passed in my run.

